## Quick Answer

Yes, in most cases you can turn your garage into a legal accessory dwelling unit (ADU) in Bellevue, Kirkland or Redmond. [Washington law](https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=36.70A.681) requires these cities to allow ADUs converted from existing structures, **"including but not limited to detached garages"**, even if your garage sits closer to the property line or covers more of the lot than today's code allows. You will still need a building permit with plan review, and the finished space has to meet the same building and energy rules as any new home.

A conversion is usually the fastest and least expensive way to add a rentable unit or a place for family, because your foundation, walls and roof are already standing. On our [ADU construction](/services/adu-construction) page, garage conversions are listed from $95,000 for about 400-500 sq ft, with 2-3 months of building once the permit is issued. Your own number depends on the slab, the ceiling height and how far new plumbing has to run.

## Is Your Garage a Good Candidate?

Walk out to your garage with a tape measure and look for five things. They decide whether your conversion is simple or expensive.

1. **Is it detached or attached?** A detached garage becomes a small backyard home of its own (a DADU). An attached garage becomes a unit that shares a wall with your house, which means the shared wall has to protect each household from a fire in the other.
2. **How high is the ceiling?** Garages are often framed lower than living rooms, and new floor insulation plus a finished ceiling take a few more inches. Measure from slab to the underside of the framing and bring that number to the first conversation with the city.
3. **What shape is the floor?** Most garage floors are a single concrete slab that slopes toward the door. Look for cracks, damp corners and water stains. Most projects add insulation and a new floor on top, and a slab that is out of level needs a leveling layer first.
4. **How far is it to your sewer line?** A kitchen and a bathroom mean new drain lines. If your garage sits at the back of a long lot, the trench to the house's side sewer can cost more than all the finishes together.
5. **Is your lot on a steep slope or near a stream or wetland?** The state ADU protections do not apply to lots in critical areas or their buffers, so on those lots the city's own rules decide what you can do.

## What You Can Count On Under State Law

Washington's ADU law sets a floor that every Eastside city has to meet. For your garage project it means:

- **You can keep the garage where it stands.** A conversion is allowed even if the garage breaks today's setback or lot coverage limits.
- **You do not have to live on site.** The city cannot require you to live in the house or the ADU, so you can rent both.
- **Parking is limited.** If you live within a half-mile walk of a major transit stop, the city cannot ask you for any off-street parking for the ADU. Elsewhere it can ask for no more than one space per unit on lots under 6,000 sq ft and two on larger lots.
- **Fees are capped.** Impact fees on an ADU cannot exceed half of what the city charges for a new house, and the city cannot make you rebuild the street or sidewalk as a condition of the permit.

Parking is the question homeowners ask first, because the cars that lived in the garage move to the driveway or the street. Whether your city wants a replacement space depends on its own code, below. For the full ADU rules across seven Eastside cities, see our [Eastside ADU rules guide](/blog/adu-dadu-rules-cost-eastside); this article stays with your garage.

## Your Project, Step by Step: Paperwork, Parking and Timing

All three cities rewrote their ADU rules in June 2025. Where you live changes a few practical things about your project, not whether you can do it.

### Paperwork: plan on a full permit

Whichever city you live in, turning your garage into a home means a building permit with plan review, not a quick over-the-counter permit. Bellevue says so in [plain words](https://bellevuewa.gov/city-government/departments/development/permits/faqs/building-permit-requirements) for converting a garage into living space, and Redmond lists garage conversions among the projects that need a building permit. In Kirkland you also sign an affidavit with your ADU application and record a covenant with King County that notes the ADU on your property.

Kirkland adds one condition worth knowing before you design: you can keep your garage exactly where it stands, even if it is closer to the line than today's rules allow, but you cannot push it any closer while you convert it ([Kirkland's 2025 ADU ordinance](https://www.kirklandwa.gov/files/sharedassets/public/v/3/planning-amp-building/cars/housing/ordinance-o-4905.pdf)). In Redmond, ask early whether a sprinkler system is required for your converted garage: the city requires sprinklers for new homes and detached ADUs, and a sprinkler system can change your water service and your budget.

### Parking: where the cars go

Once the garage becomes a home, your cars move to the driveway or the street, so ask about parking before anything else.

- **In Redmond** the adopted parking rules require zero spaces for an ADU, so in most cases you will not have to replace the garage space.
- **In Kirkland** you need none if you live within a half-mile of the STRIDE (I-405) bus rapid transit station. Farther away, a lot with more than one ADU needs one off-street space unless street parking is available within 600 ft or frequent commute-hour transit runs nearby ([Kirkland's ADU rules](https://www.kirklandwa.gov/Government/Departments/Planning-and-Building/Housing/The-Kirkland-ADU-Toolkit/ADU-Regulations)).
- **In Bellevue** confirm the requirement for your lot with the city; the state limits above still apply.

### Timing: how long the city takes

Kirkland says a standard ADU permit takes [about three months](https://www.kirklandwa.gov/Government/Departments/Planning-and-Building/Housing/The-Kirkland-ADU-Toolkit/Planning-Your-ADU); its faster pre-approved plans are for new cottages, so your conversion goes the standard route. Bellevue aims to finish its first review of a single-family permit in [21 days](https://bellevuewa.gov/city-government/departments/development/performance-strategy/permit-review-timelines) and each correction round in 14, and most projects need at least one round. In Redmond, once your permit is issued it stays valid for a year, and each passed inspection extends it by another year.

### Size: room to grow, or build on top

Your ADU can be up to 1,200 sq ft in Bellevue and Kirkland and up to 1,000 sq ft in Redmond, far more than most garages offer. If you would rather keep parking downstairs, Bellevue allows a detached ADU built above an existing garage up to 28 ft tall instead of the usual 24 ft ([Bellevue pre-approved plans](https://bellevuewa.gov/city-government/departments/development/codes-and-guidelines/pre-approved-plans)). Homeowners in Kirkland can find more local detail on our [Kirkland ADU page](/services/adu-construction/kirkland).

## What Changes Inside Your Garage

The permit lets you start; the work inside is where your money goes. In a garage conversion that comes down to four pieces of work.

### Turning a cold garage into a warm home

Your garage is unheated today. Once it becomes a home, the converted space has to meet [Washington's energy code](https://app.leg.wa.gov/WAC/default.aspx?cite=51-11R-50200) in full, just like a new house. In practice you insulate the walls, the ceiling and the floor, replace the big garage door with an insulated wall and windows, and add heat and fresh-air ventilation. This is usually the largest single block of work after plumbing.

### A safe way out of every bedroom

Every bedroom needs a window or door you can climb out of in a fire, opening to the [yard or street](https://app.leg.wa.gov/WAC/default.aspx?cite=51-51-03100). As Bellevue summarizes it, the window needs at least 5.7 sq ft of clear opening, at least 24 inches high and 20 inches wide, with the sill no more than 44 inches above the floor. The old garage door opening gives you a whole wall to work with, so this is rarely a problem, but it decides where your bedroom goes.

### Protecting both households in an attached garage

If your garage shares a wall with the house, the wall and ceiling between the two homes normally need a **1-hour** fire rating, and any door between them must be a self-closing, fire-rated door. Washington [relaxes this](https://app.leg.wa.gov/WAC/default.aspx?cite=51-51-0302) for one ADU created within an existing house when every smoke alarm in both units is interconnected. Ask the plan reviewer which path applies to your house before the drywall is priced.

### Alarms and fresh air

New bedrooms bring the alarm rules for new homes: a smoke alarm in each bedroom, one outside the sleeping area and one on each level, and [wireless alarms](https://app.leg.wa.gov/WAC/default.aspx?cite=51-51-0314) that all sound together are allowed. [Carbon monoxide alarms](https://app.leg.wa.gov/WAC/default.aspx?cite=51-51-0315) go outside the sleeping area and on each level.

## What a Garage Conversion Costs

There is no city price list for this kind of work, and online averages mix Seattle with rural counties. What we can say:

- **Your starting point.** A conversion reuses what you already have, which is why it starts well below a new detached cottage (around $180,000 on our ADU page for a 400 sq ft studio).
- **What moves the price up.** Long or deep sewer runs, a slab that needs leveling or moisture work, a low ceiling that means lowering the floor or raising the roof, a new electrical service, and a sprinkler system where the city requires one.
- **What keeps it down.** A detached garage with a sound slab, close to the house's existing drain line, with power already run to it.
- **Fees as their own line.** Permit fees are based on project value, and impact fees are capped at half of a new house's. Ask your contractor to show them separately in the quote.
- **What it can earn.** On our ADU page we estimate rent for a converted garage studio at about $1,500-$1,800 a month in the Seattle area, depending on neighborhood, finishes and parking.

Every number above is a starting range, not a quote. Call for a quote based on your garage after a site visit.

## Timeline: From First Call to Move-In

1. **Site visit and measurements** (1-2 weeks): ceiling height, slab, sewer route, electrical panel, setbacks.
2. **Design and drawings** (several weeks): floor plan, energy code path, fire separation for attached garages.
3. **City review:** Kirkland estimates about three months for a standard ADU permit; Bellevue's targets are 21 days for the first review and 14 days per revision cycle.
4. **Construction** (about 2-3 months for a typical conversion): framing the door opening, plumbing, electrical, insulation, drywall, kitchen and bath.
5. **Final inspection** and the unit is ready to live in or rent.

The permit stage is the part you can speed up: complete drawings and early answers on parking, sprinklers and fire separation save whole review cycles.

## Questions to Ask Before You Sign

- Does the quote include the permit, plan review and impact fees, or are they separate?
- Who draws the plans and who answers the city's correction letters?
- How will the slab, the ceiling height and the sewer connection be handled, and what happens to the price if the slab needs work?
- For an attached garage: which fire separation path does the plan use?
- Where will the cars go, and does the city require a replacement parking space on this lot?

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Can I convert my garage into an ADU if it is too close to the property line?

Usually yes. State law requires cities to allow ADUs converted from existing structures, including detached garages, even if they violate current setback or lot coverage rules. Kirkland adds one condition: the conversion cannot make the existing nonconformity worse.

### Do I need to replace the parking I lose?

It depends on the city and your lot. Redmond's adopted parking table requires zero spaces for an ADU. Kirkland requires none near the STRIDE station and in some cases one space elsewhere. State law forbids parking requirements within a half-mile walk of a major transit stop and caps them everywhere else.

### Do I need a permit to turn a garage into living space?

Yes. Bellevue requires a permit with plan review for converting a garage into living space, and Redmond lists garage conversion to living space among the projects that need a building permit. Kirkland also requires an ADU permit and a recorded covenant.

### Can I keep the garage and build an ADU above it?

Yes, that is a common alternative. Bellevue allows a detached ADU built above an existing garage up to 28 ft tall instead of the usual 24 ft. It costs more than a conversion because it adds new framing and a roof, but you keep the parking.

### How long does a garage conversion take?

Construction of a typical conversion takes about two to three months. Add design and city review: Kirkland estimates about three months for a standard ADU permit.

